<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gorgobina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gorgobina was a town of the Boii in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, given to them by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> after the Boii joined the Helvetian migration and were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 58 BCE. In 52 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> besieged it to punish the Boii for their loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, drawing Caesar to its relief. Its exact location in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> is uncertain.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Gorgobina is named only by Caesar, who calls it the oppidum of the Boii but never their capital, and it is not known whether they held any other stronghold. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> had settled the Boii on the edge of their own territory, near the frontier with the Bituriges. At the beginning of 52 BCE, during the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> besieged the town, hoping to force Caesar to come to the relief of his Boian dependants and so to expose himself.</p><p>Caesar marched to its aid, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Cenabum</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnutes">Carnutes</a> and Noviodunum of the Bituriges on the way, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> raised the siege when he heard of his approach. The episode drew Caesar into the campaign in the land of the Bituriges that culminated in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaricum">Avaricum</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Caesar give the name in two forms, Gorgobina and Gortona, and his text implies only that the town lay in Aeduan territory. In 1866 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> placed it at Sainte-Parize-le-Châtel, near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the Allier, a view later taken up by Thomas Rice Holmes, while Camille Jullian favoured La Guerche. In 1904 Jacques Soyer argued for Sancerre, whose early medieval names included Gorthonas and Cortono castro, and in 1960 Émile Thévenot supported him by calculating the marching distances in Caesar's narrative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sancerre, or more precisely the riverside commune of Saint-Satur, has since gained a measure of agreement, but the identification is not regarded as proven, and Venceslas Kruta lists La Guerche-sur-l'Aubois and Château-Gordon near Sancerre as other possible sites. No remains of the La Tène period have been found on the promontory of Sancerre or at Saint-Satur, where a Gallo-Roman settlement of the 1st to 4th centuries CE is known. Sophie Krausz suggests that a Boian settlement would more likely lie on the plain by the river and may not have had the walls of a usual Gallic oppidum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gorgobina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They received the town of <b>Gorgobina</b>, perhaps near Saint-Parize-le-Châtel.</blockquote><a href="/state/boii-of-gaul">Boii of Gaul</a></li></ul></section>
